= Introduction

== Context

This document aims to serve as a guide for writing documentation in the domain of computer science. It attempts to present useful thinking patterns, workflows, concepts, and tools that help software professionals write and maintain documentation that is consistent, accurate, and easy to understand.

Ultimately, there is one core objective that this document aims to achieve:

Writing documentation for software should be so trivially easy that there is never an excuse not to do it.

== Common Questions

=== Who is this document for?

This document is intended for the following audiences:

- Ongoing software professionals that are looking for guidance on what they should document about their software and how to do so.
- Unexpierenced or non-primarly software oriented companies that are looking to establish processes for software documentation based on best practicies
- Expierenced or Software oreiented companies that may face issues with their current documentation processes and are looking for potential alternatives.

=== How does this guide differe from other models like arc42, ..., etc.?

There are already many great models and templates for writing software documentations, like arc42, ... and similar. Some of these models are even featured within this guide when talking about the how-to's. This document, however, tries to establish both a thinking pattern when writing any sort of documentation about a software product, as well as workflows and tools that help when actually writing documentation.

Think of this document like a guided tour through a art museum. In a art museum, you might walk through diffrent historic eras of art, hearing about the era itself before taking a closer look at some specific pieces. Similary, this document will walk you through diffrent types of documentations, talking about each type in general before taking a closere look at some specific examples.

=== How is this document structured?

This document has two main parts:

+ Firstly, we will talk about documentations in general. We will discuss what type of documentations there are, what issues there commonly are with documentations and what we can do diffrently.
+ Secondly, will will look into each type of documentation in more detail, disscussing common topics regarding the specific type of documentation before giving one concrete best practices on how and what you can document.

